\begin{abstract}
The Superluminal Thermodynamics Simulation Engine (STSE-H v2.0) is a 
production-grade computational framework designed to evaluate warp-field 
metric deformation, tachyonic thermodynamics, and extended ADM 3+1 Hamiltonian 
constraints under superluminal velocity regimes. The engine integrates 
astrophysical datasets (Gaia DR3, Planck 2018, LIGO transients) directly into 
a GPU-accelerated solver architecture secured by AES-GCM-256 hardware enclaves. 
Automated warp-factor sweeping and encrypted telemetry logging enable 
high-fidelity stress testing up to Warp~3375.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Warp-field simulation, ADM 3+1 Hamiltonian, superluminal thermodynamics, 
CUDA acceleration, astrophysical datasets, exotic matter, metric deformation.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
Superluminal metric deformation requires simultaneous control of spatial 
expansion and contraction gradients, exotic stress-energy tensors, and 
Hamiltonian constraint stability. STSE-H v2.0 provides a unified simulation 
environment capable of evaluating manifold integrity under extreme warp 
conditions. The system couples real astrophysical boundary conditions with 
a high-performance Hamiltonian solver implemented in C++20 and CUDA.

\section{Hamiltonian Formulation}
The extended ADM Hamiltonian governing the STSE-H v2.0 simulation is:
\begin{equation}
H_{\text{total}} = 
\frac{1}{2}
\left(
G^{-1}_{ijkl}\,\pi^{ij}\pi^{kl}
-
\sqrt{h}\left(
R^{(3)} - 16\pi G\,T_{\text{eff}}
\right)
\right),
\end{equation}
where $G^{-1}_{ijkl}$ is the supermetric, $\pi^{ij}$ the conjugate momentum, 
$R^{(3)}$ the spatial Ricci scalar, and $T_{\text{eff}}$ the effective exotic 
stress-energy tensor. Constraint stability is monitored through a Hamiltonian 
violation index computed at each symplectic integration step.

\section{Astrophysical Dataset Integration}
STSE-H v2.0 incorporates real-world astrophysical datasets:
